WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.800
NASA plans to keep starliner in service longer shift dragon's retirement target to 2030, and change how crews reach the station. This is SpaceX Daily, episode 104. NASA intends to fly additional starliner missions before retiring the vehicle. The shift follows internal NASA planning documents reviewed this week. Dragon would continue supporting cargo and crew rotations until that point. The planning documents were part of a broader review of commercial

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:35.460
crew capabilities. This approach ensures backup options for accessing the International Space

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:41.680
Station remain available through the end of the decade. Space X postponed the next integrated flight

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:47.540
test. No new target date was released in the announcement. The delay follows ongoing vehicle

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:54.240
processing at starbase. Teams continue work on the ship and super heavy booster stack. Regulatory

00:00:54.240 --> 00:01:00.140
review remains a factor in the schedule. Processing work includes final checks on propulsion systems

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:06.060
and structural interfaces. The postponement allows additional time for hardware validation before

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:12.140
the next attempt. Gwen Shotwell noted the importance of multiple US crew vehicles during a public

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:17.620
appearance on September 18th. She did not specify timelines for Boeing's next attempt.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:23.100
The statement underscores the value of having two independent crew transportation systems.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:28.580
Shotwell's remarks were made in the context of ongoing commercial crew program operations.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:35.600
NASA and SpaceX confirmed that Crew-13 astronauts enter quarantine ahead of the planned October mission.

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:39.860
The crew will remain isolated until launch from Kennedy Space Center.

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:43.360
Dragon spacecraft processing continues in parallel.

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:48.660
The quarantine protocol follows standard procedures to protect crew health before flight.

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:52.900
The mission will mark another rotation to the International Space Station.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:58.800
Crew 12 astronauts expressed anticipation for seeing nature again after months on the station.

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.940
NASA confirmed the upcoming undocking and splash-down sequence.

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:06.680
The mission used a SpaceX Dragon capsule for transport.

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.160
Return timing depends on weather and station operations.

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:14.380
The crew has completed all assigned station tasks ahead of departure.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:19.200
Splashdown will occur in the Pacific Ocean under Dragon Recovery Procedures.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:24.700
Tesla and SpaceX took the TerraFab trademark dispute to court on September 17.

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:28.260
The filing challenges another party's use of the name.

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:33.020
Both firms seek to protect the mark for manufacturing and technology applications.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:35.100
No hearing date has been set.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:39.920
The lawsuit addresses potential confusion in industrial and aerospace sectors.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:45.420
A lawsuit alleges negligence at the XAI-linked Memphis data center site.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:48.240
The suit seeks damages from the project owners.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:51.700
Court filings were submitted on September 17th.

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:55.500
The incident occurred at the Expanding Data Center Campus in Tennessee.

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:58.880
Project owners are named as defendants in the action.

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:03.420
NASA opened accreditation for the 35th Dragon resupply mission.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:07.020
The flight will deliver supplies to the International Space Station.

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:10.620
Space X will use a Falcon 9 and Dragon capsule.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:14.060
Launch is scheduled from Cape Canaveral in the coming weeks.

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:18.100
Media credentials must be submitted through the standard NASA process.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:23.120
The mission continues the regular cadence of cargo deliveries to the orbiting laboratory.

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:31.860
Observers are tracking the Starlink 15-27 mission from Vandenberg with booster B1093 on its 17 flight

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:34.700
and a planned landing on OCIS-LY.

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:39.280
The thread notes the three-day pad turnaround as a new record for that site.

00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:43.520
Whether data remains the main uncertainty for the September 20th window.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:49.680
Vandenberg Space Force Base confirmed support for the upcoming USSF-259 launch.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:54.040
The base provided range and safety resources for the Falcon 9 mission.

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:57.120
The flight carries national security payloads.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:02.240
One thing worth watching is the Starship flight test delay because regulatory review remains

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:03.440
a factor in the schedule.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:12.760
On the AI front, GLM 5.3 identified a vulnerability in the cursor code editor that triggered a $10 billion licensing rule.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:16.820
The flaw affects how models integrate with developer workflows.

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:20.880
Cursor's parent company, AnySphere, is evaluating patches.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:26.800
The incident highlights security considerations when frontier models reach daily coding tools.

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:31.420
The vulnerability was discovered during routine model evaluation testing.

00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:36.520
Any sphere has not disclosed the exact nature of the flaw pending further review.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:41.660
The engineering angle on extending Starliner service while planning Dragon Retirement

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:45.840
centers on life support redundancy and vehicle certification margins.

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:51.840
Dragon's current design uses a trunk section for unpressurized cargo and solar arrays

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:54.300
sized for 210 day station stays.

00:04:55.260 --> 00:05:00.160
Starliner relies on a service module with hypergolic propulsion and a different heat shield

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:06.440
approach. Extending both vehicles requires separate certification paths for avionics and environmental

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:12.720
controls. The 2030 target implies roughly four additional years of dragon flights at current

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:17.960
manifest rates. That cadence keeps the vehicle within its original structural life limits,

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:22.240
while Starliner accumulates flight hours needed for longer term approval.

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:26.540
Tradeoffs appear in crew transfer time and launch availability,

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:32.580
Since each system has distinct ground processing flows, maintaining two independent vehicles

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:36.520
also spreads risk across different propellant types and recovery methods.

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:42.600
The decision ultimately rests on demonstrated reliability data from each spacecraft rather

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:44.220
than a single shared architecture.

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:49.760
From a first principles view, redundancy and crew access reduces single point failure

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:52.800
risk by distributing the mission across dissimilar hardware.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:58.300
The Magic One number here is the raw material and energy cost of building one additional

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:01.740
dragon versus one additional Starliner service module.

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:07.060
The idiot index rises when certification and testing overhead multiply the effective

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:08.320
cost per flight hour.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:14.540
Dragon solar array power budget supports longer station stays without resupply of consumables,

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:19.540
while Starliner's hypergolic system trades simplicity for different handling requirements

00:06:19.540 --> 00:06:20.140
on the ground.

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:25.680
Over four years, the cumulative flight count for Dragon would approach the original design

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:30.440
life envelope, requiring careful tracking of structural loads and thermal cycles.

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:35.760
The parallel certification tracks allow each vehicle to accumulate the flight data needed

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:40.780
for extended approval without forcing a common architecture that might compromise either

00:06:40.780 --> 00:06:41.200
design.

00:06:41.840 --> 00:06:47.540
This approach preserves the option to retire one system if reliability data diverges, while

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:51.120
while keeping the overall manifest resilient to delays in either program.

